⍝ ZZ70_Regression.tc
⍝ ══════════════════

⍝@ test3.

      ⍝ Depth-cache (≡) full-range caching + isolate_deep()-anchored
      ⍝ invalidation, 2026-09-01 (see plan.txt). Closes the depth≥2
      ⍝ caching gap left open since the original depth-limit-enforcement
      ⍝ work: compute_depth() now trusts and caches every depth 0..254,
      ⍝ with invalidation piggybacked onto the mandatory COW isolate_deep()
      ⍝ walk that every left-value form (selective specification, Pick,
      ⍝ member access) performs before mutating. The cases below target
      ⍝ ancestors *two or more levels above* the actual mutation point --
      ⍝ the case a naive "invalidate only the directly mutated value"
      ⍝ scheme misses, since an untouched intermediate ancestor would
      ⍝ keep trusting its own stale-but-cached depth and re-derive a
      ⍝ wrong (but now re-cached) answer for everything above it.
      ⍝
      )CLEAR
CLEAR WS

      ⍝ Three-level nested array: w contains u contains v. Query ≡ at all
      ⍝ three levels first (caching each), then mutate v's third element
      ⍝ from within w's own copy of v, reached via a nested Pick used as
      ⍝ the base of a bracket selective specification (ZZ49_Regression.tc-
      ⍝ style) -- two Pick-levels below w. All three cached depths must
      ⍝ go up by exactly one level each. The right-hand side is
      ⍝ explicitly enclosed (⊂(3 4)(5 6), not (3 4)(5 6) -- Bugs30
      ⍝ #13/#16): [3] selects exactly one item, and a 2-item value
      ⍝ there is no more length-1-exempt than it would be for a plain
      ⍝ A[3]←(3 4)(5 6), which already correctly gives LENGTH ERROR.
      ⍝
      v←1 2 (3 4)
      u←10 (v) 30
      w←100 (u) 200
      ≡v ◊ ≡u ◊ ≡w
2
3
4
      (2⊃2⊃w)[3]←⊂(3 4)(5 6)
      ≡w ◊ ≡2⊃w ◊ ≡2⊃2⊃w
5
4
3

      ⍝ v and u themselves are separate copies (ordinary APL value
      ⍝ semantics) and must be unaffected by mutating w's own copy
      ⍝
      ≡v ◊ ≡u
2
3
      )ERASE v u w

      ⍝ Direct member reassignment (A.B.C←V) two dot-levels deep, with
      ⍝ the enclosing namespace's own depth queried (and cached) both
      ⍝ before and after.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL SKIP_APL2   ⍝ structured variables are a GNU APL extension
      Z.a.b←1 2 (3 4)
      ≡Z ◊ ≡Z.a ◊ ≡Z.a.b
4
3
2
      Z.a.b←1 (2 (3 4))
      ≡Z ◊ ≡Z.a ◊ ≡Z.a.b
5
4
3
      )ERASE Z

      ⍝ Member reference used as the base of a Pick + bracket selective
      ⍝ specification (the member-access invalidation gap closed in
      ⍝ Prefix.cc alongside the array case above).
      ⍝
      Y.a←10 (1 2 (3 4)) 30
      ≡Y.a
3
      (2⊃Y.a)[3]←⊂(3 4)(5 6)
      ≡Y.a
4
      )ERASE Y

      ⍝ The final-assignment rules of a selective specification, LRM p.43
      ⍝ ("Once the selection expression has been evaluated, the following
      ⍝ rules govern the replacement of values. Apply the first rule that
      ⍝ holds:"), verified against GNU APL's actual implementation
      ⍝ (Value::assign_cellrefs()) -- see Bugs30 #13-#17 for the
      ⍝ conformance work that made all four hold uniformly regardless of
      ⍝ where the left-value came from (a plain array, a member, Pick, ...).
      ⍝
      ⍝ Rule 1: "If the left is a whole array, the right array replaces
      ⍝ it." -- note the right's LENGTH need not match the left's at all,
      ⍝ unlike rules 2/3 below; this is a genuine wholesale replacement.
      ⍝
      ]FILE_CTL NO_SKIP
      A←1 2 3
      (A)←4 5 6 7
      A
4 5 6 7
      )ERASE A

      ⍝ Rule 2: "If the right is a scalar (or an array with empty shape
      ⍝ when ones are removed), then the right is paired with each item
      ⍝ from the left and these rules are applied recursively."
      ⍝
      A←1 2 3
      A[1 2]←9
      A
9 9 3
      )ERASE A

      ⍝ Rule 3: "If the left and the right have the same shape (when ones
      ⍝ in the shapes are ignored), then corresponding items from the
      ⍝ left and from the right are paired" -- (V[1 2]) has shape 2, the
      ⍝ right has shape 2 1; only equal once the trailing 1 is ignored,
      ⍝ per the rule's own explicit "when ones... are ignored" clause.
      ⍝ See Bugs30 #17 (Shape::conforms_to()).
      ⍝
      V←1 2 3
      (V[1 2])←2 1⍴9 8
      V
9 8 3
      )ERASE V

      ⍝ Restriction, same page: "Pick with an empty left argument is the
      ⍝ only [selection] function that returns the whole array" -- i.e.
      ⍝ (⍬⊃A)←B falls under Rule 1 (wholesale replace, right's length
      ⍝ need not match) even though ⊃ is, in every other case, a genuine
      ⍝ selection. The LRM's own p.42 example (with ⍳0 in place of ⍬,
      ⍝ APL2 lacking a Zilde glyph) is reproduced here directly.
      ⍝
      A←'ABCD'
      (⍬⊃A)←10 20 30
      A
10 20 30
      )ERASE A

      )SIC
      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

      )CLEAR
CLEAR WS

      ]NEXTFILE
